// NEGATIVE TEST — `n @ 1 | 2` binds inside an alternative (N6-07 / N6-08).
//
// `|` binds LOOSER than `@`, so this parses as `(n @ 1) | 2` — an or-pattern one
// of whose alternatives binds `n`. That is the refusal N6-07 already carries, and
// it fires here for a reader who meant `n @ (1 | 2)`: a form the language does not
// have, because there is no grouping form for patterns.
//
// The precedence is stated at the production in grammar.ebnf. It is pinned here
// because "binds looser" is the kind of sentence a reader believes and a compiler
// has to prove.
fn main() {
    let v = match 4 {
        n @ 1 | 2 => n,
        _ => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
